hello i'm brian darby i'm professor of  material science at the university of  manchester my particular interests in  how 3d printing can be used in medicine  if you read the newspapers you might get  the impression that very soon we'll be  3d printing whole organs which can be  used for transplant surgery really i  think we are many years away from that  sort of application in medicine to print  a complete organ is a very very  complicated piece of engineering we need  to know exactly how an organ works and  even biologists don't know completely  how an organ works at every level of its  structure what we can do is we can  produce things that look like organs but  generally they don't work like organs  the best example for that would be in  repairing bones what we can do with 3d  printing is if there's a large piece  missing  we can print a replica of the piece  that's missing to fit exactly into the  body we can make it look like bone on an  internal structure sufficiently  accurately that the body will grow new  bone tissue into the printed structure  and over a period of time that print  instructor will vanish leaving a perfect  fit a really good repair
 

